{"meta":{"course":"cat100-supplement","chapter":"02","section":"03","chapter_title":"Resume, Branding, and AI Brainstorming","title":"Professional Bio Drafting","description":"CAT 100 textbook section connecting professional bio drafting to bio before/after revision with role-focus-proof, truthful evidence, and AI-use note, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","learning_objectives":[{"id":"cat100-supplement-02-03-lo-01","statement":"Construct a short professional bio using the role-focus-proof pattern, replacing generic intensifiers with one named focus and one checkable proof"},{"id":"cat100-supplement-02-03-lo-02","statement":"Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision"},{"id":"cat100-supplement-02-03-lo-03","statement":"Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions"},{"id":"cat100-supplement-02-03-lo-04","statement":"Use feedback, support requests, and revision notes to improve the artifact before sharing it"}],"concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_resume_branding_and_ai_brainstorming","cat100_supplement_professional_bio_drafting","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":3,"top_tier_hardened":false,"prereq_section_ids":["cat100-supplement/02/02"],"practice_ids":["cat100_supplement_2_3_professional_bio_drafting_artifact_signal","cat100_supplement_2_3_professional_bio_drafting_annotation_signal","cat100_supplement_2_3_professional_bio_drafting_ai_boundary","cat100_supplement_2_3_professional_bio_drafting_calibration","cat100-supplement_02_03_lo_03_direct_check"],"lo_practice_map":{"cat100-supplement-02-03-lo-01":["cat100_supplement_2_3_professional_bio_drafting_calibration","cat100_supplement_2_3_professional_bio_drafting_artifact_signal","cat100_supplement_2_3_professional_bio_drafting_ai_boundary"],"cat100-supplement-02-03-lo-02":["cat100_supplement_2_3_professional_bio_drafting_annotation_signal"],"cat100-supplement-02-03-lo-03":["cat100-supplement_02_03_lo_03_direct_check"],"cat100-supplement-02-03-lo-04":["cat100-supplement_02_03_lo_03_direct_check"]},"alignment_method":"deterministic lexical mapping v2 with direct-evidence fallback; instructor review required"},"content":"# Professional Bio Drafting\n\n<figure-block caption=\"Organizational reference model connecting Professional Bio Drafting, Evidence Based Revision, and AI Supported Learning to an evidence-based revision decision.\" conceptid=\"cat100_supplement_resume_branding_and_ai_brainstorming\" purpose=\"organizational\" source=\"ALGET original instructional diagram\" license=\"Project-authored; all rights reserved pending project license decision\">\n  <img src=\"/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat100-supplement/02-03-professional-bio-drafting.png\" alt=\"Three connected concept cards for Professional Bio Drafting: Professional Bio Drafting, Evidence Based Revision, and AI Supported Learning, followed by an evidence-to-revision transfer prompt.\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" />\n</figure-block>\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nA professional bio is the short paragraph that appears in a LinkedIn \"About\" box, a conference program, a club website, or the footer of a portfolio. Unlike a resume, which is a list, a bio is prose, and that tempts students into the worst version of the genre: a string of warm adjectives (\"passionate, driven, hardworking\") that describe no one in particular. A reader learns nothing from it because nothing in it is specific to you. This section teaches a compact structure that keeps a bio human-sounding while still carrying checkable content.\n\nThe skill matters because a bio is often the first full sentence a stranger reads about you, and it sets expectations a recruiter or collaborator will test. Eye-tracking research shows the first screen is fast and selective: recruiters' initial scans run on the order of seconds and concentrate on a few high-value fields (Ladders, 2018; Pina et al., 2023), so a front-loaded focus and proof get read while buried virtues do not. A bio that names a real focus and one concrete proof invites a useful conversation; a bio of adjectives invites a polite nod and a click away. Your deliverable is a before-and-after bio revision, with an AI-use note, that trades generic warmth for specific evidence.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Construct a short professional bio using the role-focus-proof pattern, replacing generic intensifiers with one named focus and one checkable proof.\n- Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.\n- Use feedback, support requests, and revision notes to improve the artifact before sharing it.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nPriya needs a two-sentence bio for her student-club officer page. Her first draft reads, \"Priya is a passionate and dedicated student who is always eager to learn and make a difference.\" It is friendly and completely interchangeable; her co-officer's draft says almost the same thing with a different name. When a faculty advisor asks \"what do you actually work on?\", Priya answers in five seconds: she runs the club's accessibility-testing nights and got the campus app's contrast fixed. That answer, the specific work, never made it into the bio.\n\nThe case isolates the central problem of bio writing: the genuinely interesting facts get crowded out by reflexive adjectives. The fix is not to sound less friendly; it is to let one concrete activity carry the warmth that the adjectives were trying to fake.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nA strong short bio follows a three-part pattern that keeps it both readable and specific: **role, focus, proof.** *Role* situates you (year, major, or current position). *Focus* names the one area you want to be known for, narrow enough to be meaningful (not \"technology\" but \"data visualization\" or \"front-end accessibility\"). *Proof* is a single concrete accomplishment a reader could ask you about. The pattern works because it front-loads identity and then immediately backs it with something checkable, so the bio earns trust in two sentences.\n\nThe contrast that organizes the whole section is generic versus specific language:\n\n| Generic (cut it) | Specific (keep it) | Why the swap works |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| \"passionate about tech\" | \"focused on data visualization\" | Names a real subfield |\n| \"lots of experience\" | \"built a Tableau dashboard for a 30-person org\" | A reader can ask about it |\n| \"results-driven team player\" | \"led the club's accessibility-testing nights\" | Ownership and a real activity |\n\nThe point is not to delete personality. It is to recognize that specificity is what makes a bio sound like a person instead of a template. Polish (smoother transitions, a nicer verb) cannot rescue a bio that names no focus and offers no proof, because there is nothing underneath the polish for a reader to hold onto.\n\n## Worked Example: Drafting a Bio With the Role-Focus-Proof Pattern\n\nA strong short bio follows a simple three-part pattern: **who you are, what you focus on, one concrete proof.** Watch it built from a weak draft.\n\n**Step 1. The weak first draft.**\n\n```\nI am a passionate, results-driven student who loves technology and is\nalways looking to grow and learn new things every single day.\n```\n\nNothing here is checkable. Swap in any name and it still fits.\n\n**Step 2. Gather raw facts the student can defend.**\n\n| Slot | Fact |\n| --- | --- |\n| Who you are | Sophomore, Management Information Systems |\n| Focus | Data visualization |\n| Proof | Built a Tableau sales dashboard for a 30-person student org |\n\n**Step 3. Assemble the pattern, in first person for a LinkedIn About.**\n\n```\nI am a sophomore studying Management Information Systems, focused on data\nvisualization. Last semester I built an interactive Tableau dashboard that\ntracked event sales for a 30-person student organization.\n```\n\n**Step 4. Audit against the pattern.** Sentence opens with the role (sophomore, MIS), names the focus (data visualization), and lands one concrete proof (Tableau dashboard, 30-person org). Every adjective from the weak draft is gone, and a reader now has something specific to ask about.\n\n**Step 5. Note the AI boundary.** If an AI draft, whether from a general assistant (ChatGPT, Claude, Gemini, or Microsoft Copilot) or LinkedIn's own AI writing assistant for the About section, had added \"five years leading cross-functional teams,\" the student strikes it (a sophomore has no such record) and logs the removed claim. These tools are profile-drafting helpers, not fact sources, and their models and outputs change month to month, so the disclosure should name the tool and the date you used it.\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Build a bio with role-focus-proof\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"Draft: \\\"I am a passionate, results-driven student who loves technology and is always looking to grow.\\\" What is wrong with it?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"It is too formal\",\"Nothing is checkable; swap any name in and it still fits\",\"It is too long\",\"It names a focus\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"It fails the swap test. With only adjectives and no role, focus, or proof, it describes no one in particular.\"},{\"prompt\":\"You fill the three slots from real facts. Which set is correct for this student?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Role: passionate; Focus: technology; Proof: hardworking\",\"Role: sophomore in MIS; Focus: data visualization; Proof: built a Tableau dashboard for a 30-person org\",\"Role: expert; Focus: everything; Proof: years of experience\",\"Role: student; Focus: growth; Proof: eager to learn\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Role situates you, focus names one narrow area, and proof is one concrete accomplishment a reader can ask about.\"},{\"reveal\":\"Assembled: \\\"I am a sophomore studying Management Information Systems, focused on data visualization. Last semester I built an interactive Tableau dashboard that tracked event sales for a 30-person student organization.\\\"\"},{\"prompt\":\"An AI adds \\\"five years leading cross-functional teams.\\\" What do you do?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Keep it, it sounds senior\",\"Strike it and log the removed claim, because a sophomore has no such record\",\"Reduce it to three years\",\"Move it to the focus slot\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Strike and log. Keep the tool’s smoother phrasing only where it stays true; any claim your real record cannot back gets cut.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nBefore you publish a bio, test it against the three slots and one honesty check.\n\n1. **Role:** Does the first clause say who you are right now (year, major, or position)?\n2. **Focus:** Is there exactly one named area, narrow enough that a reader could picture it?\n3. **Proof:** Is there one concrete, checkable accomplishment, not a category of accomplishments?\n4. **Swap test:** Could a classmate paste their name over yours and have it still read true? If yes, it is too generic, and the proof slot is doing no work.\n\nA bio that passes all four can stay short; that is a feature, not a shortfall. Padding a passing bio with extra adjectives moves it backward.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"match\" items='[{\"term\":\"Role\",\"definition\":\"Situates you right now: year, major, or current position\"},{\"term\":\"Focus\",\"definition\":\"Exactly one named area, narrow enough that a reader can picture it\"},{\"term\":\"Proof\",\"definition\":\"One concrete, checkable accomplishment, not a category of them\"},{\"term\":\"Swap test\",\"definition\":\"If a classmate could paste their name over yours and it still reads true, it is too generic\"}]'></sequence-builder>\n\n<inline-check question=\"Which phrase belongs in the FOCUS slot rather than being cut as generic?\" explanation=\"&quot;Front-end accessibility&quot; names a real, narrow subfield a reader can picture and ask about. &quot;Passionate about tech,&quot; &quot;hardworking,&quot; and &quot;results-driven&quot; are intensifiers that stand in for a fact instead of naming one.\" options='[{\"text\":\"\\\"focused on front-end accessibility\\\"\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"\\\"passionate about tech\\\"\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"\\\"hardworking and dedicated\\\"\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"\\\"results-driven team player\\\"\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nOne misreading is to assume a bio must be long to seem accomplished. Length is not credibility; a vague long bio is just a generic short bio with more filler. Two specific sentences beat a paragraph of warmth.\n\nA second misreading is to let an AI tool (a general assistant such as ChatGPT, Claude, Gemini, or Microsoft Copilot, or LinkedIn's built-in AI writing assistant) inflate the role to make it sound senior. AI bio generators love phrases like \"seasoned professional\" and \"years of experience leading teams,\" which are false for most first- and second-year students. The fix is the same as for a resume: keep the tool's smoother phrasing only where it is still true, and strike any claim your real record cannot back.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nBuild a bio for a sophomore who tutors writing. Start from the weak draft: \"Sam is a motivated student passionate about helping others succeed.\" It hits all three failure modes at once, no role, no focus, no proof, just adjectives. Fill the slots from real facts: role is \"second-year English major\"; focus is \"writing support for multilingual students\"; proof is \"tutors about ten students a week at the campus writing center and led a workshop on thesis statements.\"\n\nAssemble in the role-focus-proof order: \"Sam is a second-year English major focused on writing support for multilingual students. At the campus writing center, Sam tutors about ten students a week and recently ran a workshop on building clear thesis statements.\" Run the swap test: another tutor could not paste their name over \"led a thesis-statement workshop\" and the weekly count, because those are Sam's specific facts. The bio is now short, warm, and entirely checkable, which is the standard the section is teaching.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nA strong reader treats \"passionate,\" \"driven,\" \"results-oriented,\" and their cousins as flags rather than content, and asks of each one: what specific fact was this adjective trying to stand in for? Then they replace the adjective with that fact. Reading the section well means leaving with a sharper ear for empty intensifiers.\n\nA strong reader also picks one focus on purpose, even when they have several interests. Trying to be known for everything reads as being known for nothing. Choosing the single focus that fits the audience is a judgment call, and making it deliberately is part of the skill.\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nThe role-focus-proof pattern scales up and down. A one-line conference name-badge tagline forces you to keep only the focus and the strongest proof; a longer \"About\" page lets you add a second proof or a line about where you are headed. The constraint that changes is space and audience: a recruiter's first scan rewards the focus up front, while a community-club page might lead with the shared mission. If you can compress or expand the same true facts to fit the slot without resorting to adjectives, you have understood bio drafting rather than memorized one paragraph.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the bio before/after revision with role-focus-proof, truthful evidence, and AI-use note packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat100-supplement/02-03-professional-bio-drafting-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"bio before/after revision with role-focus-proof, truthful evidence, and AI-use note\" course=\"CAT 100\" section=\"02.03\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[NACE career readiness](https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the bio before/after revision with role-focus-proof, truthful evidence, and AI-use note.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the bio before/after revision with role-focus-proof, truthful evidence, and AI-use note for CAT 100 02.03. A peer comment points to an adaptive move issue connected to [ISTE digital citizenship](https://iste.org/standards/students). Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"Which clue should guide what support or revision comes next?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The combination of the note, artifact evidence, confidence, and revision quality.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"1\" conceptid=\"cat100_supplement_professional_bio_drafting\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, make sure your trace links claim, evidence, tool boundary, revision, and remaining limitation. That is what separates digital fluency from simple completion.\n\n## Concept Map\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat100_supplement_professional_bio_drafting\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## References\n\n- [NACE career readiness](https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined)\n- Pina, A., et al. (2023). Using machine learning with eye-tracking data to predict if a recruiter will approve a resume. Machine Learning and Knowledge Extraction. https://doi.org/10.3390/make5030038\n- [ISTE digital citizenship](https://iste.org/standards/students)\n- data-ink and visual evidence. https://www.edwardtufte.com/tufte/books_vdqi\n- [WCAG accessibility](https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/)\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"cat100_supplement_2_3_professional_bio_drafting_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_professional_bio_drafting","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_professional_bio_drafting","cat100_supplement_resume_branding_and_ai_brainstorming"],"stem":"A professional bio for a LinkedIn \"About\" section should usually be written in which voice, and why?","options":["Third person (\"Maria is a junior studying...\") because it always sounds more formal.","First person (\"I am a junior studying...\") because LinkedIn is a personal profile where readers expect you to speak for yourself.","Second person (\"You are reading about Maria...\") to address the recruiter directly.","Whatever the AI tool chooses, since voice does not affect how a bio reads."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A LinkedIn About section is your own profile, so first person reads as natural and direct. Third person is common for speaker bios or website team pages, but on a personal profile it can feel stiff or ghost-written.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_2_3_voice_is_decorative","difficulty":"easy","learning_objective_ids":["cat100-supplement-02-03-lo-01"]},{"id":"cat100_supplement_2_3_professional_bio_drafting_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_professional_bio_drafting","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_professional_bio_drafting","evidence_based_revision"],"stem":"A bio's first sentence reads: \"I am a passionate, results-driven, detail-oriented self-starter.\" What is the main problem with this opening?","options":["It is too short to be a real bio.","It stacks buzzwords that describe almost anyone and tell the reader nothing concrete about what you do.","It uses first person, which is wrong for a bio.","It should begin with your full legal name."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Buzzword stacks (\"passionate, results-driven, detail-oriented\") are interchangeable across thousands of profiles. A strong opening names a role, a field, and one concrete thing you do.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_2_3_buzzword_opening","difficulty":"medium","learning_objective_ids":["cat100-supplement-02-03-lo-02"]},{"id":"cat100_supplement_2_3_professional_bio_drafting_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ai_supported_learning","concept_ids":["ai_supported_learning","cat100_supplement_professional_bio_drafting"],"stem":"You ask AI to draft your bio and it produces a polished paragraph describing \"five years of industry experience leading cross-functional teams.\" You are a second-year student. What should you do?","options":["Keep it; a confident bio gets more attention.","Keep the experience claim but change five years to three.","Rewrite it to reflect your actual level, such as coursework, a club role, and one project, and note the AI claim you removed.","Submit it as-is and explain in the interview that AI wrote it."],"correct_index":2,"explanation":"A bio is a truthful self-description. AI happily invents seniority you do not have. Replace fabricated experience with real, level-appropriate detail and log what you removed.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_2_3_ai_invents_seniority","difficulty":"hard","learning_objective_ids":["cat100-supplement-02-03-lo-01"]},{"id":"cat100_supplement_2_3_professional_bio_drafting_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_professional_bio_drafting","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_professional_bio_drafting","evidence_based_revision"],"stem":"Which bio opening best follows the pattern \"who you are + what you focus on + one concrete proof\"?","options":["\"I am a hard worker who loves to learn and grow every day.\"","\"I am a CAT 100 student who is interested in many areas of technology and business.\"","\"I am a sophomore studying management information systems, focused on data visualization; last semester I built an interactive sales dashboard in Tableau for a 30-person student org.\"","\"My name is Jordan and I am currently a student at the university.\""],"correct_index":2,"explanation":"The strong opening names the role (sophomore in MIS), the focus (data visualization), and one concrete proof (a Tableau dashboard for a 30-person org). The others stay generic or never get to evidence.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_2_3_no_concrete_proof","difficulty":"medium","learning_objective_ids":["cat100-supplement-02-03-lo-01"]},{"id":"cat100-supplement_02_03_lo_03_direct_check","type":"conceptual","stem":"Provide a concise response that demonstrates this learning objective: Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.","expected_answer":"The response accurately demonstrates the section objective: Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions, using relevant section concepts, evidence, or calculations.","explanation":"This direct-evidence item is used when no closed-response item adequately samples the objective. Score it with the section concepts and worked examples as the rubric.","difficulty":"medium","learning_objective_ids":["cat100-supplement-02-03-lo-03","cat100-supplement-02-03-lo-04"]}]},"misconceptions":[{"id":"cat100_supplement_2_3_voice_is_decorative","pattern":"voice_choice_is_decorative","description":"Believing the choice between first and third person in a bio is cosmetic rather than tied to the platform and audience","trigger":"learner picks a voice at random or lets a tool decide without considering where the bio will appear","feedback":"Match voice to the venue. First person fits a personal LinkedIn About; third person fits a speaker or team-page bio.","rail_action":"explain"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_2_3_buzzword_opening","pattern":"buzzword_stack_opening","description":"Opening a bio with stacked adjectives (passionate, results-driven, detail-oriented) that fit almost anyone","trigger":"learner's first sentence is a string of personality adjectives with no role or proof","feedback":"Open with a role, a focus, and one concrete thing you have done, not a list of interchangeable adjectives.","rail_action":"represent"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_2_3_ai_invents_seniority","pattern":"accepting_ai_invented_seniority","description":"Keeping AI-generated claims of experience or leadership the student does not actually have","trigger":"learner accepts a bio that overstates years of experience or scope of leadership","feedback":"A bio must be true. Replace invented seniority with your real level: coursework, a club role, or one project.","rail_action":"ask"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_2_3_no_concrete_proof","pattern":"bio_without_concrete_proof","description":"Writing a bio that states interests and goals but never includes one concrete, checkable accomplishment","trigger":"learner's bio lists what they like but names no project, role, or measurable result","feedback":"Anchor the bio with one proof: a project, a tool you used, or a number, so the reader has something specific to remember.","rail_action":"practice"}],"content_version":{"content_version":"af29d4cbf8fd60758974e2a76880745110f70d38181adc18a411d9466d93274b","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}